A||||6||westtexas||Can '88 rear brake calipers be put on wrong side?||||1||
Z||000000||westtexas||09-15-2001||09:37 PM||||I recently bought an '88 coupe in AZ. It has several things I need to fix before I can register it, the ebrakes being one. My problem: when the ebrake lever is released the right rear rotor is locked; when the ebrake is pulled tight the right rear rotor turns freely. The left brake rotor spins freely all of the time, the ebrake doesn't affect it. From what I understand there is an actuator screw that tightens the caliper for the ebrake. Either there is a right hand and left hand threaded screw on the wrong side or the calipers are on the wrong side. Any advice on this will be greatly appreciated.<P>Robert Jennings<p>[This message has been edited by westtexas (edited 09-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||09-15-2001||10:11 PM||||Quick way to check if calipers are on the wrong side is to look at the bleeder screws both should be on the top, they can be put on the wrong side( don't ask me how I know).<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_1.gif">  <BR>SC 3800||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||09-15-2001||11:14 PM||||Check the calipers as Phil said.  The e-brake screws inside the pistons turn in different directions.  Having a caliper meant for the other side srue sounds like a problem.  There also may be frozen cables, or other problems if one wheel won't brake at all.<P>BTW, if you take the calipers off, be sure to pound out the pad retaining pins from the BACK side.  If you pound them out from the front, it is a LOT harder!  The pressure holding them in is from the back holes, not the front.<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2m6siga.JPG">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-15-2001||11:41 PM||||As stated above, the bleeder screw should be on top.<P>It is posible the wrong piston and screw was put into a rebuilt caliper. In that case the only fix is a replacement piston and screw. the easiest way to get that is another caliper.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||westtexas||09-16-2001||12:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>Quick way to check if calipers are on the wrong side is to look at the bleeder screws both should be on the top, they can be put on the wrong side( don't ask me how I know).<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The bleeder screws are on top on both sides and the cables move freely. When I tightened the left hand caliper lever as far as I could with cannel locks it never engaged the rotor. On the right hand side, after I tightened the lever about 3/4" the rotor was able to move; when I let go of the lever it locked the rotor again. I'm going to assume (as dangerous as that is) that the prior owner botched rebuilding the calipers. Guess I'll take them apart and see if I can switch the actuator screws. Does anybody have a brand preference for rebuilt '88 calipers? Thanks!<P>Robert Jennings<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-16-2001||03:08 PM||||If you take them apart, at the very least you'll need seal kits for them.||||reg||1
Z||000006||westtexas||09-16-2001||06:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>If you take them apart, at the very least you'll need seal kits for them.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, I am going to take them apart as I don't see that I have a lot to lose. It seems rebuilts are very expensive and/or poorly rebuilt. Thanks for mentioning the seal kit, I'll get one. If I'm not any better mechanic than the last owner I'll invest in rebuilts. I'm humbled fairly regularly. I put the star wheels in my '78 Chevy pickup in the wrong rear wheels and instead of tightening the brakes when I backed up, the backwards threads loosened the shoes. Changing the master cylinder didn't fix the problem, some thought and looking at the shop manual finally did. I feel honored to have theogre answer a question, I'm not sure that have enough time in this lifetime to learn as much as you do about our Fieros.<P>Robert Jennings<BR>Plumber today||||reg||1
